<h2>An unusual route to a POCSO FIR</h2>
<p>This FIR did not begin with a complaint by the woman or her family. It began with a complaint case filed by an NGO, in which an inquiry was ordered; the inquiry concerned a young woman brought dead to the AIIMS trauma centre in December 2024, with a suicide note recovered. The FIR under Section 6 POCSO followed on 3 July 2026 — about a year and seven months after the death. The applicant was her husband, having married her in September 2023 while his earlier marriage subsisted; the FIR proceeded on the premise that she was a minor when the relationship began.</p>

<h2>Two documents, two different ages</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>School record</h3>
    <p>Date of birth: 1 January 2007 — which would make her under eighteen at the time of the September 2023 marriage, attracting POCSO.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">v.</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>Aadhaar card &amp; parents&rsquo; affidavits</h3>
    <p>Date of birth: 1 January 2005 — which would make her an adult, taking the case outside POCSO altogether.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>Ordinarily, the juvenile justice framework gives primacy to the school certificate over other documents. But primacy in the final determination is not the same as conclusiveness at the threshold. Justice Mini Pushkarna, relying on the Supreme Court&rsquo;s decision in <em>State of U.P. v. Anurudh</em>, 2026 SCC OnLine SC 40, held that the question of age is one for trial; at the bail stage the court takes only a prima facie view of the competing documents. Here, that prima facie view was that the records raised genuine doubt about the victim&rsquo;s minority — and with it, doubt about the applicability of POCSO itself.</p>

<h2>Conduct under interim protection</h2>
<p>The Court also weighed the applicant&rsquo;s conduct: interim protection had been granted earlier, and he had joined the investigation under it. Cooperation during interim protection is often the deciding makeweight in anticipatory bail matters, because it answers the State&rsquo;s standard objection that custodial interrogation is necessary. The interim protection was accordingly confirmed, with the usual conditions on arrest: a personal bond of Rs 10,000 with one surety, reporting to the investigating officer, no contact with the complainant side, and no tampering with evidence.</p>

<h2>The larger point for POCSO practice</h2>
<p>Age-dispute cases sit at the fault line of POCSO: the statute&rsquo;s severity is justified by the victim&rsquo;s childhood, so where minority itself is genuinely doubtful on the documents, courts are careful before applying its full rigour at the pre-trial stage. Three practical consequences follow. First, both sides should assemble the complete documentary record on age — school admission register, matriculation certificate, birth certificate, Aadhaar — at the earliest stage, because the bail court&rsquo;s prima facie view is formed on whatever is placed before it. Second, a delay in registration of the FIR, as here, invites scrutiny of how the case came to be constructed. Third, nothing at the bail stage settles the age question: the trial court will decide it on evidence, unaffected by the bail order&rsquo;s observations, which the Court expressly confined to the application before it.</p>
<div class="note">Anticipatory bail is governed by Section 482 of the BNSS, 2023 (formerly Section 438 CrPC). Section 6 of the POCSO Act punishes aggravated penetrative sexual assault. This article is informational; whether POCSO applies in a given case depends entirely on the evidence of age in that case.</div>
